Confirmed the bug: with throwOnInvalid, `Interval.fromISO('2007-03-01T13:00:00/P1Y2M10DT2H30M')` throws InvalidDateTimeError because `DateTime.fromISO(e)` is called on the duration string first, and DateTime.fromISO throws when throwOnInvalid is set. The correct behavior: the duration branch should be attempted, and the resulting error should be an InvalidIntervalError (or at least the valid parse should succeed).

The real upstream fix (luxon commit "fix throwOnInvalid logic for Interval" / later refactor) rewrote fromISO to parse the pieces more carefully. Let me recall the actual upstream code (luxon 1.21+):

```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed as ISO 8601`);
  }
```

