arguments.report:
## Root cause

`Interval.fromISO` (src/interval.js) unconditionally parsed **both** halves of the input as DateTimes:

```js
const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts), end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
```

For the valid ISO-8601 interval forms `<start>/<duration>` and `<duration>/<end>`, one half is a *duration*, so `DateTime.fromISO` on it returns an invalid DateTime — and with `Settings.throwOnInvalid = true` it **throws `InvalidDateTimeError`** instead. That exception escaped `Interval.fromISO` before the duration branch (`Duration.fromISO` → `Interval.after`/`Interval.before`) could run, and before the fallback `Interval.invalid("unparsable", …)` (which correctly throws `InvalidIntervalError` under `throwOnInvalid`) could run. Net effect: with `throwOnInvalid` on, valid duration-based intervals threw `InvalidDateTimeError` ("works with Settings.throwOnInvalid" fails), and the wrong error type/behaviour leaked out of `fromISO`.

## Diff applied (only file changed: /luxon/src/interval.js, `fromISO`, lines 134-169)

```diff
   static fromISO(text, opts) {
     const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
     if (s && e) {
-      const start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts),
+      let start, startIsValid;
+      try {
+        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
+        startIsValid = start.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
+        startIsValid = false;
+      }
+
+      let end, endIsValid;
+      try {
         end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
+        endIsValid = end.isValid;
+      } catch (e) {
